==Latvian==

===Etymology 1===
From an unattested verb {{term/t|bat-pro|*balt||to become white}} (of which {{term|balts|lang=lv}} originally was the [[past participle]] form; cf. {{etyl|lt|-}} verb {{term|bálti|lang=lt}}, and cf. {{etyl|lv|-}} 17th-century derived verb {{term|baltīt||to make, paint something white|lang=lv}}, later replaced by other verbs, derived from {{term|balts|lang=lv}}: from {{etyl|bat-pro|lv}} {{term/t|bat-pro|*bal-}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|lv}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰol-||shiny, white}}. Cognates include {{etyl|lt|-}} {{term|báltas|lang=lt}}, {{etyl|xsv|-}} {{term|baltas|lang=sv}}. In several Indo-European languages, reflexes of the stem {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰel-}}, {{term/t|ine-pro|*bʰol-}} are often found in words relating to water or humid places, probably due to their shiny, reflective surfaces: {{etyl|xil|-}} {{term/t|xil|*balta}} (“marsh, swamp”), {{etyl|sq|-}} {{term|baltë||mud, sludge, swamp|lang=sq}}, Proto-Slavic {{term/t|sla-pro|*bolto||swamp, lake}} ({{etyl|cu|-}} {{term|блато||lake|tr=blato|lang=cu}}, {{etyl|ru|-}} {{term|болото||marsh, swamp|tr=bolóto|lang=ru}} (dialectal “puddle, lake”), {{etyl|cs|-}} {{term|bláto||mud; pl. swamp|lang=cs}}, {{etyl|pl|-}} {{term|błoto||mud, swamp|lang=pl}}). This usage is also attested in Baltic languages, as in, e.g., {{etyl|prg|-}} placename {{term|Namuynbalt|lang=prg}} (swamp). It left also traces in Latvian, in the names of lakes or swamps (''Baltenis'', ''Baltiņa purvs''), and is a possible source of the word {{term|balti||Balts, Baltic|lang=lv}}.<ref name="LEV"/>

