===Etymology 2===
The mainstream opinion on this word is that it comes from old uses of the stem {{term|balts||white|lang=lv}} ([[q.v.]]) in names of places containing water, giving rise to the name of the Baltic Sea (already {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|Mare Balticum|lang=la}}); it is possible that {{term/t|bat-pro|*balt-}} in {{etyl|prg|-}} placenames referred originally to the sea or coastal area. Another suggestion is that {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|Mare Balticum|lang=la}} is to be derived from {{etyl|non|lv}} {{term|balti|lang=non}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|balte|lang=da}}, from {{etyl|la|lv}} {{term|balteum||belt|lang=la}} (whence {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Belt||sea strait|lang=de}}), perhaps because of this sea's narrowness, or because of the many islands and straits between islands and the continent.<ref name="LEV">{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

